      title: "dptexas.com"
    }
    summary: "For plumbing systems, air pressure tests can often be substituted for water tests, with specific pressure requirements and conditions depending on the type of system and local codes. This method is particularly useful when water cannot be used, such as in cold environments or during early construction phases[1].\n\nKey details for air pressure testing include:\n*   **Drainage and Vent Systems** For drainage and vent systems, an air test typically involves pressurizing the system to 5 psi (34.5 kPa) or enough to balance a 10-inch (254 mm) column of mercury. This pressure should be maintained for at least 15 minutes[2][3][4]. Some codes, like the International Plumbing Code (IPC), specify that plastic piping should not be tested using air for drainage and vent systems due to safety concerns[2]. However, local amendments might permit it in certain situations, such as in cold climates where water testing is impractical[4].\n*   **Water Supply Systems** For water supply systems, an air test of not less than 50 psi (344 kPa) can be used for piping systems other than plastic. This pressure must be held for at least 15 minutes[5][6][7]. If testing a system that will operate at a higher working pressure, the test pressure should not be less than the working pressure of the system[5][6].\n*   **General Considerations**\n    *   **Safety** Air is compressible, and high-pressure air tests can be dangerous if a system fails, potentially causing an explosive release of energy. Water, being incompressible, does not pose the same risk[8][9]. For this reason, many codes and manufacturers prohibit air testing for plastic piping systems[2][9].\n    *   **Effectiveness** A low-pressure air test (e.g., 30-50 PSI) can be as effective as a high-pressure water test for detecting leaks because air has lower viscosity and surface tension than water, allowing it to escape through smaller openings[8]. Air testing can also make it easier to locate leaks by listening for hissing sounds or using leak-detection solutions[10].\n    *   **Gauges** When performing low-pressure air tests, it\'s important to use a gauge that can accurately show pressure in smaller increments[4][11].\n    *   **Preparation** Before an air test, all openings in the system must be tightly sealed, typically using appropriate test plugs[12][11]. The system is then pressurized using an air compressor, and the pressure is monitored with a gauge[1]. Any drop in pressure indicates a leak[13][14].\n\nSources:\n[1] [lessopipe.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH-9BzUncW7z-DKdCFbIxdDAqiqF38oF_tcO1NNAQIp9CPJ3aEXl4i-vJVbb3jr7CcXg8DI-rMru8fLsnY4mlmDn09O4DubmqB_SYgA7FRIfpUCrddIs7USaLp2f-B_PPYrIDxpTaVA3JuIhy9ju6NRVcvxtT0=)\n[2] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE0YSOLkdgdg2qw1OZkUA-u5EpUoyUknWWJjjxoAqSukzchKwcWGgPLlgItSo1JKvHrycS2dT73eSxnePGBYUzkjFshMabJkCNVrH4EQ__fmDpr5rFpHkOmobtabptxIG0L7_TM8jQOzO59LJ3mQGOseKtnNddAZl1WAbJqxZYAiyo5XMhlzntMFjl7X_nsKN-YqPexZA==)\n[3] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHhmnDGwyIo47o7dBDhsmc-eqbVr3SL3CPciblJxamnWHGWiz9WhokTdSW-1jBr-BXcc8AD4IrlGv_dGpcg7C5QDfwocmY3Rx1o1zKqMdFAV7C_8vNb2vawTMlOQe4FnNx6Z2bQcE_ufygwmQ02-MJVk7NTQjf5NdgjTF9inMZOLLOIW-qmxmO8SXljzmX_ro33TpbrRw==)\n[4] [youtube.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGa8QysYtzdUbsfASQln3gn3CdAmesV8L-d5JeVB66_nzxjRVkstGt4WcPihOQRgoAiuep7Ko0l7SKQph6hzupTLekMa7sJJkh6GKb_tnesVFC4FGmf1z7QBL-VyObPy4_0amii5Q==)\n[5] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE4VdzVwCvZOiPuofdTNKJzkXW7RZEYP4Eb5yhuqBXGC01PBAeNPZd0YLD86WJMXpCQgP3MGlKye-WnCTGvDyyKPt4znPrujVJC-R2snSp-16Y4rm-hoVVBDKafW-5GN6MzSkfJhLCa7fdl6AbYL4xQNxZpjScmT9df9hWr8TAaClKRoylo6i84kmX6VkPfmm_O)\n[6] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFAv913Hj7gVbfDu90YY67hl2oFmoU5YMBzZn5kF0KgYQIZKJ9cQKhonJ9QYcc