    summary: "When testing a water piping system, an air pressure test can often be substituted for a water (hydrostatic) test, particularly for piping systems other than plastic. The specific air pressure required varies depending on the applicable plumbing codes and the type of piping.\n\nCommonly cited air pressure requirements for water piping systems include:\n*   **50 psi (344 kPa)** For piping systems *other than plastic*, many codes, including the International Plumbing Code (IPC) and International Residential Code (IRC), allow an air test of not less than 50 psi, held for a minimum of 15 minutes.[1][2][3][4]\n*   **100 psi** For water piping in new manufactured homes, the system can be tested with air or water at 100 psi for 15 minutes without pressure loss. Similarly, the North Carolina Plumbing Code specifies a minimum of 100 psi air pressure for testing the entire water supply system, including distribution and service piping, held for at least 15 minutes.[5][6]\n\n**Important Considerations:**\n*   **Plastic Piping:** Some codes and manufacturers advise against high-pressure air testing for plastic piping systems (such as PVC, CPVC, and ABS) due to safety concerns. Air is compressible and stores significantly more energy than water, leading to a risk of explosive failure if the system ruptures.[7][8][9][10] For PEX piping systems, compressed gas testing is only permitted if specifically authorized by the manufacturer and not prohibited by other codes.[11]\n*   **Safety:** While air tests can detect leaks, lower air pressures (e.g., 30-50 psi) are often considered as effective for leak detection as higher water pressures due to the different properties of air and water (viscosity and surface tension).[7][12] High-pressure air testing can be dangerous because compressed air can propel objects with significant force if there is a system failure.[7][12][8]\n*   **Local Codes:** Always consult local plumbing codes and the manufacturer\'s instructions for the specific piping materials being installed, as requirements can vary.[4][11]\n\nSources:\n[1] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGfNxriQXDPPAMXbVObm3KTc9mBAV0tJRc2YgVAEmLX-HqNHYz0SZv6y_qrgTwcBCGikyRpfSKQoPbMBL8yT4XPYSMkeTM4qw9ccubs45ceGUB5AB4f3CFPjfkqxbJFZznilcPB4lVoIFEmYyrhvltmKkzfWz6apHORJXcwVWE5xB_aGTA3-VmgOlNCRV6cFIlTwVJmvQ==)\n[2] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFOaP11TS6pHgtaoyw53n8_p6D9xS3k1Bq_8jZQ54Q-bB-M-BxlGhi_2RGzFQ4Z1mt-1T1QZDMosOcZxKCwZUKFNvvwqc5E99G1N32GPn_-f1u7idj91PMArnfi78l3A_q3JsqCGtzRvkKhUt5H-2V6ogTZldTJKjEoMs94tMjny4WO__iJPP7t9sOaQcbwITqE)\n[3] [iccsafe.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHyWjG_Sb3p8Ib0gYZu5UhVTKB0tKhPDrLZSNz_n7s3cVcb3LAWBoqFn5lLmlakagLwN5puFmSXXzMSql2wv8AbOgHNWID8BDWhxBYGE3_tlpAERUqJA-Wl3vO2sdx_K40kC_jQ6XEGE3jvvxSUMSWk-T7Ilrmf8x1PHeoO2JCU5uNv3SWyGqRriex8ZR6g9xXP83t2Q3Gox8tNN65hZ8I=)\n[4] [stackexchange.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF3W6kvXkqBmOCipGFnyX-lWtPE5KkSnffq4WByJyU6rv9XOBLrus-rwfksQtcv_9ESgExhIkQyTLMY9qJ42CSNnCxYz3wxAuOlRRxnnrzuS5dLnAqaKURGlnoKXvNxlOYBkaZ2J1hbTUS54E8Gj6nANkb49WqmOYn00yq-voOW6dPv_0xCGxM0SXXY)\n[5] [nclicensing.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHSc-odiehXfPbaDy13XufeUvwLWUEICix8Gb1cD08eWP8ZqEHp5946BGeXIRbz1uMD9p0V7cwvuPhbBMIbNiLXjVjHV7HWmWVSSR5ET7xsFBMxCFUCB5KRU0bYhRIxvo4-ImqysGZXl8JEdTSVgvSyu50Sge3AgJSkyeY_9-IpyDB0J-WWtq7BOsmB7YHix-6ZqED_7en7qJt-LQ==)\n[6] [ncosfm.gov](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEzsISwWxuEQZsc5uyBFGgUpixfFO56VFesx1ihgpQyZDMNN5Aw4fCCNCo9Xmg6JE5GSScfk7SFxCEzPES8ViP4YCsoo61OHerRZmoWXW3miEyCb69WE0ozZL_CrhHs_vDBTZ9fNaR5SrCBqSj0kJgUQ3gPZb9ZCPepaO--Rg==)\n[7] [griswoldcontrols.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHkcdMx-WhWeC3MtyNG91dnSRdTCldy2C9H0q7uL2UqC_h9WbhOVzF3qpQwT1Qour-NJal7e10nPDzGzVAlhipq-F8