uracy of all the questions. The final question list is now completely refined, and ready for script creation.\n\n\n**Refining Question Integration**\n\nI\'m now methodically adding the final questions, making certain to avoid duplicates and ensure accuracy. I\'ve finished integrating all questions, and have reviewed the data for any potential duplicate entries. I\'ve compiled the final, refined dataset for script creation, and verified it.\n\n\n**Finalizing Question List**\n\nI\'ve completed integrating these final questions and meticulously avoided duplicates while maintaining accuracy. My refined, verified list is now ready for the script; the data set is completely ready for script generation.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing Question List**\n\nI\'ve now incorporated the final elements and completed integrating the question set, meticulously checking for and preventing duplicates. I\'m focusing on validating the accuracy of all the questions to verify the list. The final question list is now fully refined, validated, and ready for script creation.\n\n\n**Verifying Packing Material**\n\nI\'m now focusing on verifying the non-combustible packing material, correctly identifying it as Oakum, and not a guide that holds molten lead, which is a joint runner. I\'ve re-examined the context of the question to confirm my understanding of both the guide and the packing material.\n\n\n\n**Refining Question Integration**\n\nI\'m now methodically adding the final questions, making certain to avoid duplicates and ensure accuracy. I\'ve finished integrating all questions, and have reviewed the data for any potential duplicate entries. I\'ve compiled the final, refined dataset for script creation, and verified it.\n\n\n**Finalizing Plumbing Knowledge**\n\nI\'m now identifying that a hygrometer measures humidity, and 34.5 kPa air pressure substitutes for water testing. With all information consolidated, I\'m now ready to address all questions and move forward with the next stage of this assessment.\n\n\n**Confirming Concepts and Terms**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a new set of questions related to plumbing concepts. I\'ve correctly identified the best valve for throttling (Globe Valve), the unobstructed top edge of a fixture (Flood Level Rim), and the correct hot water demand for a private hotel lavatory (2 GPH). I\'ve also identified the phase transition of gas to solid as Deposition, and the fitting used in a yoke vent (Wye fitting). I am making a last check on this set before proceeding to the next.\n\n\n**Verifying Yoke Vent Details**\n\nI\'m verifying that a wye fitting is indeed the correct component at the lower end of the yoke vent, as per plumbing code. I\'m also now analyzing the question on the closet bend and that it must be cut off smooth with the top of the closet ring. I\'ve also correctly identified Chemical Oxygen Demand, and the solids found at the bottom of a septic tank.\n\n\n**Validating Final Questions**\n\nI\'m now methodically reviewing and validating the final question set to ensure complete accuracy and a comprehensive grasp of the plumbing principles covered in the prompt. I\'ve now correctly identified the answers for three new questions: \"Jar Test\" is correct, and \"Receptor\" and \"Barb\" are correct, and 100 ml is correct for hardness testing. I\'m verifying all responses.\n\n\n**Refining Pipe Knowledge**\n\nI\'m now identifying that the standard length for PVC pipes in the Philippines is 3 meters, for both sanitary and other uses, based on code. I also understand that a jockey pump maintains system pressure, and viscosity describes internal liquid friction. Furthermore, I correctly identified an area drain assembly installed in a basement.\n\n\n**Refining Brass Materials**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed that the primary composition of brass pipes consists of 15% zinc and 85% copper, aligning with industry standards. Furthermore, I\'ve identified a pressure-reducing valve as the correct device for managing and maintaining consistent fluid pressure in a system. I\'m now exploring the concept of ductility in relation to plumbing materials.\n\n\n**Assessing Remaining Questions**\n\nI\'m now identifying that the soil pipe is commonly listed by weight, as \"Service Weight\" or \"Extra Heavy\". Further, I\'ve confirmed that the correct measurement of heat equivalent is the Therm, equivalent to 100,000 BTU, and the WSFU for a bedpan washer is 10. I also know that the minimum hanger rod size for 1/2\" to 4\" pipes is 3/8\".\n\n\n**Refining Quality Criteria**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on who establishes drinking water quality standards. I\'ve cross-referenced the Sanitation Code (PD 856) and the National Standards for Drinking Water (PNSDW). The Department of Health sets criteria per PD 856 and it uses PNSDW as standards. I\'m choosing \"National Standards for Drinking Water\" as the most accurate.\n\n\n**Finalizing Question Solutions**\n\nI\'m now identifying that \"Class I System\" is correct for standpipe connections. I also confirm that pendent, conventional, and upright sprinklers are accurate for identifying fire sprinklers.\n\n\n**Refining Sprinkler Identification**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the fire sprinkler identification. I\'ve re-evaluated and confirmed my understanding. I\'ll transform it into three distinct questions for increased usability and user-friendliness.\n\n\n**Refining Answer Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now methodically verifying the question responses, including the correct SI unit for temperature (Kelvin), the Provincial Water Utilities Act (PD No. 198), and the valve part depicted in the image. I have also correctly identified the rod (Sacrificial) in the water heater, and I\'ll proceed to the remaining questions.\n\n\n**Confirming Knowledge Recall**\n\nI\'m now methodically verifying several concepts. I\'ve correctly recalled that the critical level in a flow prevention device without markings is the bottom of the vacuum breaker. I confirmed that 1 horsepower is 746 watts and the atmosphere