  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Exam State Investigation**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into how the app determines exam status. It appears we\'re using local storage to track this. I\'ve identified three potential keys: `LS_KEY_EXAM_STARTED`, `LS_KEY_TIMER_DURATION`, and `LS_KEY_TIMER_END_TIME`. The first seems like a boolean flag, while the latter two store the duration and end time of the exam timer, respectively.\n\n\n**Defining Exam State Variables**\n\nI\'m now identifying and defining the necessary variables for managing the exam state. I\'ve declared `examStarted`, `timerDuration`, `timerEndTime`, and `timerInterval`, along with their corresponding local storage keys. I\'m also working on a `startExam` function, which handles the transition to an active exam state, including time calculations, local storage updates, and UI changes. I\'m also thinking of edge cases for the `startExam` function, especially if the exam is already submitted.\n\n\n**Refining Screen Navigation**\n\nI\'m refining the logic for bypassing the start screen. If `isGraded` is true, the workspace should be shown immediately, since the exam is already complete. I\'m updating the `init()` function to include this logic, along with restoring the `examStarted` state from local storage. My focus is on ensuring a smooth transition to the workspace for both active and graded exams. I\'m also ensuring the correct states for UI display.\n\n\n**Designing Timer Functionality**\n\nI\'m now designing the `hideTimerUI()` and `startTimerTicker()` functions. `hideTimerUI()` will hide the timer container in the header and the timer card in the sidebar. `startTimerTicker()` will show both, and set up an interval to tick down the timer every second. When `timeLeft` reaches zero, I\'ll clear the interval and automatically submit the exam.\n\n\n**Implementing Timer Rendering**\n\nI\'m now implementing the timer rendering logic within `startTimerTicker()`. I\'ll directly trigger the submission or use `document.getElementById(\'submit-test-btn\').click()` to simulate the submit button click once the timer reaches zero. Otherwise, I\'ll format the remaining time for display in both header and sidebar, and I\'ll also calculate and update the progress bar width, considering edge cases with `Math.min` and `Math.max`. Finally, I\'ll implement a visual alert (red/amber) when `timeLeft` is less than five minutes.\n\n\n**Adding Clear Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing the functionality to clear exam progress, as well as a more elegant user experience to trigger this feature. I\'m adding a \"Clear Exam\" button to the start screen. Clicking the button calls a new `clearAllProgress()` function that removes all relevant keys from `localStorage`, specifically `LS_KEY_ANSWERS`, `LS_KEY_IDX`, etc. I\'m thinking of adding a short-duration visual cue to confirm progress reset, instead of a disruptive dialog box, likely changing the button text to \"Cache Cleared!\" for 2 seconds. This function is designed to reset the app state seamlessly.\n\n\n**Refining Progress Reset**\n\nI\'m now refining the progress reset. Besides clearing local storage, I also need to reset in-memory variables like `userAnswers`, `isGraded`, `currentIdx`, `examStarted`, and `timerEndTime`. I\'ll show \"Progress Reset!\" on the button for feedback, and call `buildIndicesMap()` to ensure a fresh start. Furthermore, I\'ve confirmed that clicking submit, or the timer expiring, should trigger `stopTimerTicker()`.\n\n\n"
