    summary: "The Provincial Water Utilities Act of 1973, also known as Presidential Decree No. 198, is a Philippine law enacted on May 25, 1973[1][2]. Its primary purpose is to establish a national policy favoring local operation and control of water systems[3][2][4]. The Act authorized the formation of local water districts to own, operate, and manage water supply and distribution systems for domestic, industrial, municipal, and agricultural uses, as well as to provide wastewater collection, treatment, and disposal facilities[3][1][5][4]. It also chartered the Local Water Utilities Administration (LWUA), a national agency providing technical and financial support to these local water districts[3][1][5][4]. The Act aims to address the shortcomings of existing water utilities in provincial areas by encouraging the formation of independent, locally controlled public water districts[1][5].\n\nRegarding the required fire reserve percentage according to the Green Building Code, a search of relevant green building codes, such as the California Green Building Standards Code (CALGreen) and the International Green Construction Code (IgCC), did not yield specific information about a \"required fire reserve percentage\"[6][7][8][9][10]. While these codes address various aspects of sustainable construction, including energy efficiency, water conservation, and indoor environmental quality, and the IgCC emphasizes general fire and life-safety concerns, a specific \"fire reserve percentage\" was not found in the provided search results[8][10]. Fire reserve requirements are typically covered under general building codes or fire codes rather than specifically within green building codes.\n\nSources:\n[1] [lawphil.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEuJOIm2mFT-lje-xPvrruK9D5IidOVw3hGjEu08PWEq7xm1pzkukkAgiPqWqYyxsB0dCb01x7gLEf3GVcn-AAp2EEgDKr5YZIzu5dmYLtZaiqhvhKKkmUBFATMcn0P1muWaUFHIuvoJ0bjhz6pn2ADkvc-lImvzw==)\n[2] [ppcwater.gov.ph](https://vert