  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing Image Generation Limitations**\n\nOkay, so I hit a roadblock with the image generation tool \342\200\223 a pesky quota error! My initial reaction was, \"Now what?\". I quickly referenced the `image-generation/SKILL.md` instructions, specifically the \"Prefer known assets\" section. This seems like a critical direction. It would appear that this constraint will be crucial to progress.\n\n\n**Optimizing Image Rendering Strategy**\n\nI\'ve been exploring a new rendering strategy. I\'m excited about the possibility of using inline SVGs instead of external images! This approach could allow for beautiful, responsive diagrams directly within the buttons. I\'m focusing on rendering illustrations such as a union, a bushing, and a nipple, rendered with custom design and crisp detail.\n\n\n**Designing SVG Components**\n\nI\'m incredibly enthusiastic about this SVG approach! It\'s solid! I\'m planning out three SVG components: a coupling, a valve with a packing callout, and a set of five fire sprinkler head profiles. My new focus is on implementation: storing these SVGs in `/src/questions.js`, likely under a new `svg` property within questions or choices. This ensures they\'re integrated seamlessly with the existing structure.\n\n\n**Implementing SVG Display Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on integrating the SVGs. I\'ve realized I can define an `svgs` array for each question, containing the SVG strings corresponding to the choices. The exciting part is I\'m testing the `index.html` file to see how it can render inline SVG strings. It looks very promising! I can conditionally render SVGs using `renderActiveQuestion`, checking for the `svgs` property and extracting the corresponding SVG string.\n\n\n**Refining SVG Rendering Logic**\n\nI\'ve been working on refining the SVG rendering logic! I am incredibly excited about this. I\'ve designed the SVG illustration for the bushing question choices, as a union. I\'m now working on implementing this illustration, with specific focus on SVG components and integrating them smoothly into the questions.\n\n\n**Generating SVG Components**\n\nI\'m now generating SVG components. I\'ve been focusing on three illustrations: a coupling, a hex bushing, and a pipe nipple. The plan is to create them in HTML, then render them using an SVG tag. This would enable easy embedding into my question display logic. I\'m aiming for clean, scalable, and visually clear representations for each component.\n\n\n**Finalizing SVG Illustrations**\n\nI\'ve got the SVG components finalized! I\'ve rendered a hex bushing and a coupling. The focus now is integration. I\'ll insert these directly into the `/src/questions.js` file, aiming for a clean structure under each question\'s `svgs` property. I\'m really happy with the look and feel of the drawings!\n\n\n**Designing Valve SVG**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the SVG for \"Identify the part of the valve: Packing,\" and I\'m really happy with how clean it is. I\'ve designed a clear illustration of a valve, with the packing component clearly highlighted. The code\'s clean and concise, and the design\'s detailed, which allows me to proceed efficiently.\n\n\n**Designing Valve Diagram**\n\nI\'ve just added the SVG code for a valve diagram, specifically highlighting the packing. I am happy with the clean and well-defined illustration. I\'ve designed the valve with precise details. It\'s now ready for immediate integration into the existing content.\n\n\n**Illustrating Fire Sprinklers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the fire sprinkler illustrations! I\'ve designed the SVG for the three fire sprinkler head styles, rendering them as clearly labeled A, B, and C: Upright, Pendent, and Conventional. I\'ve designed a clear SVG for this, and I\'m quite happy with the design. Now, I\'ll integrate it into the `questions.js` file.\n\n\n**Refining Sprinkler Illustrations**\n\nI\'ve been further refining the sprinkler illustrations, working specifically on the \"Pendent\" style. I\'ve been detailing the base and thread, aiming for a more realistic and visually clear rendering. I\'m focusing on lines and rectangles in the SVG to show the thread. I\'m aiming for crisp detail.\n\n\n**Integrating Sprinkler Design**\n\nI\'ve been working to integrate the \"Pendent\" fire sprinkler style, and I\'ve focused on detailing the base, thread, and bulb. I\'m excited with the rendering as an SVG component. I\'m now integrating this into the existing data structure, with the goal of adding an additional Sprinkler \"B\" choice to the questions array.\n\n\n**Finalizing Sprinkler Integration**\n\nI\'ve finalized the integration of the fire sprinkler illustrations! I\'ve confirmed that the SVG matches the correct answers in `questions.js` perfectly. It\'s now rendering correctly, exactly as I planned. The illustration is breathtakingly beautiful and highly technical, providing all three styles: A, B, and C styles of fire sprinklers.\n\n\n**Adding New Question**\n\nI\'m now adding a new question to address a user\'s request: \"identify the parts of the bulb\". I\'ve decided to incorporate a question about \"Identify the parts of the fire sprinkler bulb:\". This will allow me to design an SVG diagram featuring a glass sprinkler bulb, labeled with components like the glass shell, temperature-sensitive liquid, and vapor bubble. I am currently formulating the SVG design.\n\n\n**Designing Bulb Components**\n\nI\'m now in the design phase for a new question about identifying parts of a fire sprinkler bulb. I am particularly excited, as the user request is a great match for my SVG illustration approach! I\'m planning a detailed SVG diagram, with labels for the glass shell, the temperature-sensitive liquid, and the all-important vapor bubble. I\'m focusing on the clarity of the design, especially the vapor bubble component.\n\n\n"
