    thinking: "**Discovering Image Locations**\n\nI\'ve just realized a critical issue: the AI Studio environment doesn\'t store user-uploaded images as files in the expected locations like `/app`. I was initially perplexed by the absence, but now I understand they\'re handled differently, residing within the multimodal context of the chat itself. This changes how I need to approach any image-related analysis!\n\n\n**Investigating Image Retrieval**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to reliably access user-uploaded images in the chat context. I\'ve confirmed that these images aren\'t automatically written to the file system as I originally anticipated. I\'m exploring if we can directly utilize the `generate_image` tool to handle these images, or whether the image data is embedded within the prompt attachments. I\'m trying to figure out if it is possible to extract these images from the user prompt or original files! I\'m also now considering the possibility that image files could be served via URL, and trying to look through the test files for clues about how images are being referenced.\n\n\n**Analyzing File Origins**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! I realized the image file names suggest they originated from a downloaded Google Form webpage. Specifically, the structure matches when you save a Google Form as a \"Webpage, Complete,\" which creates an HTML file and a corresponding \"_files\" folder containing the images. This points to the user potentially uploading an HTML file, or possibly a GitHub repository with an HTML file. The key now is confirming if the user uploaded the HTML, the folder, or both. Further investigation of the context is needed to determine the exact upload method, particularly the mention of a requested \"action\" that might provide more clues about what was submitted.\n\n\n**Examining Image References**\n\nI\'ve just examined `/src/questions.js`, and found image references that were, in fact, imported into the original system. These specific files w