  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Clarifying User Instructions**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `window.questionsData` is indeed an array. However, I\'ve had to stop and re-examine the user\'s instructions. It seems I must not supplement questions or add illustrations, nor generate AI questions, and must stick to the images provided. It\'s a key constraint.\n\n\n**Investigating File Modifications**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focused on understanding file modifications and how the questions are integrated. I\'m investigating if the user directly embedded the questions within `/index.html` or if they are still sourced from `src/questions.js`. Based on the file structures, it seems the questions are loaded from a separate script file. I\'m checking the file history to determine the previous state of `/index.html` and understand how the questions were included initially.\n\n\n**Analyzing File Updates**\n\nI\'m now carefully analyzing the file updates to understand the user\'s workflow. It appears the initial HTML upload contained the questions directly. Then, a previous agent extracted them to `src/questions.js`. I had assumed a raw HTML re-upload, but the current `index.html` actually still references `window.questionsData`. I\'m now examining the file title, and the lines that references the window.questionsData for further validation to confirm this.\n\n\n**Locating Uploaded HTML**\n\nI\'m now back-tracking, trying to locate the user\'s HTML. The recursive search of `/app/applet/` yielded nothing new, which means the file isn\'t in a standard location. I\'m focusing now on the possibility of a missed commit from a previous agent or, more likely, a previous message containing the HTML itself. The provided context indicates the user stated \"I added the html again.\" The conversation history is now the prime target for review.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating User Expectations**\n\nI\'m now reconsidering the implications of the user\'s instructions regarding questions 77-100. I am going to search in `questions.js` to determine if they are AI-generated. I am checking for the original 100 questions to see if I can find those in the HTML. I\'m also confirming I shouldn\'t supplement, add illustrations, or use AI-generated questions. The focus is to stick with the provided HTML and images. I will focus on finding those original 100 questions.\n\n\n**Identifying Image Sources**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! The \"images I uploaded\" definitely refers to the six image files provided. The user wants me to stop using the custom-generated SVGs in `src/questions.js` and instead use these uploaded images. I\'ve located several IDs that reference SVGs in the file. Now, I need to figure out where the original images are.\n\n\n**Mapping Image Relationships**\n\nI\'m now mapping the relationships between question IDs and the provided image files. I\'ve successfully linked several IDs to specific JPG files based on the file paths. I\'m focusing on clarifying whether the question IDs and corresponding images align with my initial understanding, to ensure proper referencing. I\'m specifically confirming whether the sprinkler bulb question uses unnamed(6).jpg.\n\n\n\n**Accessing Image Attachments**\n\nI\'m now focused on understanding how to access the image attachments directly from the conversation. I\'ve realized the images aren\'t written to disk, which means they are only available within the LLM\'s visual context. I need to figure out how to extract the image content from the current context. It seems I must find a way to access those images in their current visual representation in order to include them.\n\n\n**Implementing Image Embedding**\n\nOkay, I\'ve had a significant breakthrough. If the images exist within the visual context of our prompt, I can encode these images as base64 data URLs. This will allow the images to render directly in the live preview and also be saved as actual image files when the content is exported as a ZIP. I now know the content and names of the images